Who is the most important person in the kingdom?
At that time the disciples came to Jesus. They asked him, "Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?"
Jesus called a little child to him, and he set him in the midst of them.
Jesus said, "Truly I tell you, unless you change and become like little children, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven."
Whoever humbles himself like this child is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven.
Whoever accepts a little child like this for my name, accepts me.
But woe to the man who causes one of these little ones who believe in me to sin! It would be better for him to have a great millstone hung around his neck and to be drowned in the depths of the sea.
How terrible it will be for the world because of the things that drive people to sin! Such things must happen. But how terrible it will be for those who cause them!
If your hand or foot causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one leg or one arm than to be thrown into the hell where their fire never goes out.
If your eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into hellfire. The story of the lost sheep.
Look, do not despise any of these little ones. Here is what I say to you. Their angels in heaven always see the face of my Father who is in heaven.
.<.>
What do you think? Let's suppose a man has a hundred sheep, and one of them goes missing. Won't he leave the ninety-nine sheep on the hillside? Won't he go and search for the lost sheep?
What I am going to tell you now is the truth. If he is found that sheep, he will rejoice over it more than his rejoicing over the other ninety-nine that did not go astray.
As well as your father who is in heaven. He does not want even one of these little ones to perish. When someone sins against you
If your brother wrongs you, go to him. Tell him what he did. Keep it between yourselves. If he listens to you, you have won him back.
But what if he will not listen to you? Take one or two others along with you. The Bible says: "Every matter should be established by the testimony of two or three witnesses." (Deut. 19:15)
But what if he also refuses to listen to witnesses? Then report it to the church. But what if he refuses to listen even to the church? Then do not treat him as a brother. Treat him as you would treat someone who is not a believer or a tax collector.
What I am determined to say is the truth. What is closed on earth is closed in heaven. And what is opened on earth is opened in heaven.
Again, here is what I tell you. If two of you agree on earth about anything you ask for, it will be done for you by my Father who is in heaven.
Where two or three are gathered in my name, I am there among them." A servant who showed no mercy.
Peter came to Jesus and asked, "Lord, how many times must I forgive my brother when he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus answered, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times."
The parable of the Kingdom of Heaven is like a king who wanted to settle accounts with his servants. He called them and asked what they owed him.
When the king started doing that, a man who owed him millions of dollars was brought to him.
The man was unable to pay. So he gave his master an order. The man, his wife, his children, and everything he possessed were to be sold to pay off his debt.
The servant fell to his knees before him. "Give me a reprieve," he pleaded. "I will make everything right."
His master felt sorry for him. He forgave him the debt he owed and let him go.
But this servant went out later and found one of the other servants who owed him some dollars. So he seized him and began to strangle him. "Pay what you owe!" he said.
The other servant fell on his knees. "Give me a deadline," he pleaded. "I will pay you."
But the first servant refused. Instead, he went and ordered the man to be imprisoned until he could repay his debt.
The other servants saw what happened. This greatly disturbed them. They went and told their master everything that had happened.
Then the master called the first servant. He said, "You wicked servant." "I forgave you all that you owed me because you begged me."
Should you not have had mercy on the other servant, as I had mercy on you?
In a rage, his master handed him over to the jailers. And he will be punished until he pays back everything he owes.
Thus shall my heavenly Father treat each of you, unless you from your heart forgive your brother.
Jesus teaches about divorce.
And when Jesus had finished all these sayings, he departed from Galilee and went into the region of Judea beyond the Jordan River.
Large crowds followed him. And he healed them there.
Some Pharisees came to test him. They asked him, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for any and every reason?"
Jesus answered, "Haven't you read that he who created them from the beginning made them male and female?" (Genesis 1:27)
He said, "Therefore a man shall leave his father and his mother, and be joined to his wife; and they shall become one flesh." (Genesis 2:24)
They are no longer two, but one. Therefore, let no one separate what God has joined together.
They asked, "So why did Moses command that a man divorce his wife, write her a certificate of divorce, and send her away?"
Jesus answered, "Moses permitted you to divorce your wives because of the hardness of your hearts. But it was not like this from the beginning."
Here is what I am telling you. Any man who divorces his wife and marries another woman commits adultery. A man is not allowed to divorce his wife unless she has been unfaithful to him.
The disciples said to him, "If the relationship between husband and wife is like this, it is better not to marry."
Jesus answered, "Not everyone can accept the idea of celibacy. Only those who have been given the ability to live without marriage are those who can accept it."
Not all men are able to procreate because they were born that way. And some were made that way by others. And others made themselves that way in order to serve the Kingdom of Heaven. Whoever can accept living that way, let them do so." They brought to him little children.
Some people brought their young children to Jesus. They wanted him to place his hands on the children and pray for them. But the disciples told the people to stop.
Jesus said, "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them, for the kingdom of heaven belongs to such as these."
Jesus laid his hands on them. Then he left there. Jesus and the Rich Young Man.
A man approached Jesus and asked him, "Teacher, what good thing must I do to inherit eternal life?"
"Why do you ask me about what is good?" Jesus answered. "There is no one good but One, that is, God. If you want to enter into life, keep the commandments."
"Which?" the man asked. Jesus said, "Do not murder. Do not commit adultery. Do not steal. Do not give false testimony."
«Honor your father and your mother.» (Exodus 20:12-16; Deuteronomy 5:16-20) and «You shall love your neighbor as yourself.» (Leviticus 19:18)
"I have obeyed all those commandments," said the young man. "What else do I have to do?"
Jesus answered, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell your possessions and give to the poor. You will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow me."
When the young man heard this, he went away sad. He was very rich.
Then Jesus said to his disciples: "Truly I tell you, it is hard for a rich person to enter the kingdom of heaven."
Again I tell you: It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for someone who is rich to enter the kingdom of God."
When the disciples heard this, they were truly amazed. They asked, "Then who can be saved?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "What is impossible with people is possible with God."
Peter answered him, "Behold, we have left everything and followed you! What then will be the reward that will be given to us?"
Jesus said to them, "What I am about to tell you is true. When all things are renewed, the Son of Man will sit on his glorious throne. Then you who have followed me will also sit on twelve thrones. You will judge the twelve tribes of Israel."
Everyone who has left houses or family or fields for my sake will receive a hundred times more and will inherit eternal life.
But many of the first shall be last; and many of the last shall be first.
The story of the workers in the vineyard.
The parable of the Kingdom of Heaven is like a man who was a landowner, who went out early in the morning to hire laborers for his vineyard.
He agreed to pay them the usual wage for a day's work. Then he sent them into his vineyard.
At about nine o'clock in the morning he went out again. He saw others standing in the marketplace doing nothing.
He said to them, "Go also and work in my vineyard, and I will pay you what is right."
So they left. "He went out again around noon and at three o'clock and did the same thing."
Around five o'clock he went out and found others standing around them. He asked them, "Why have you been standing here all day without working?"
They answered, "Because no one has hired us." He said to them, "Go also you, and work in my vineyard."
When evening came, the owner of the vineyard said to the manager of the workers, "Call the workers and pay them their wages. Begin with the last ones hired, and then go to the first ones."
The newly hired workers arrived around 5 o'clock. Each of them received their usual day's wage.
When the time came, the first who had been hired thought, "Surely we will receive more." But each one of them also received the normal daily wage.
When they took it over, they started complaining about the owner.
They said, "These people you just hired worked only one hour." "You paid them the same you paid us. We did most of the work and were under the hot sun all day."
The employer answered one of them. He said, "My friend, I am dealing with you fairly. Did you not agree to work for the usual day's wage?"
Take your money and leave. I want to give those whom I have recently hired the same wage that I gave you.
Do I not have the right to do what I want with my own money? Do you feel betrayed because I have generously donated to others?
The last will be first, and the first will be last. Jesus also speaks of his coming death.
Jesus was going up to Jerusalem. And on the way, he took the twelve disciples aside to talk to them.
He said, "We are going up to Jerusalem. The Son of Man will be betrayed to the chief priests and teachers of the law. They will condemn him to death."
Then they will hand him over to people who are not Jews. And the people will mock him and beat him. And they will crucify him on a cross. And on the third day, he will rise from the dead!" Or a mother asks a favor of Jesus.
The mother of my sons Zebedee came to Jesus. Her sons came with her. She knelt down and asked him a favor.
"What is it you want?" Jesus asked her. And she said, "Promise me that one of my sons will sit at your right in your kingdom. And promise that the other will sit at your left."
Jesus said to them, "You do not know what you are asking." "Can you drink the cup that I am to drink?" They answered, "We can."
Jesus said to them, "Truly, you will drink from my cup. But how, I do not say, who will sit at my right hand or at my left. These places are for those whom my Father has prepared."
The other ten students heard about this. They became angry with the two brothers.
Jesus gathered his disciples. He said, "You know about the rulers of the Gentiles. They lord it over their people. And their high officials exercise authority over them."
Do not be like this. Rather, whoever wants to be great among you must be your servant.
Whoever wants to be first must be your servant.
Become like the Son of Man. He did not come to be served, but to serve and give his life as a ransom for many.' Two blind men receive their sight.
Jesus and His disciples were leaving Jericho. A great crowd followed Him.
Two blind men sitting by the road heard that Jesus was passing by. They called out, "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on us!"
The crowd ordered them to stop. They told them to be silent. But the two men cried out even louder: "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on us!"
Jesus stopped and called to them, "What do you want me to do for you?" he asked them.
They said, "Lord, we want to see."
Jesus felt deep concern for them. He touched their eyes. And they immediately regained their sight. And they followed him.
Jesus enters Jerusalem.
And behold, all of them approached Jerusalem, and they came to the villages of Bethphage, which was on the Mount of Olives. And Jesus sent two disciples, saying to them, "Go into the village opposite you, and immediately you will find an ass tied, and a colt with her. Loose them and bring them to me."
He said to them, "Go to the village before you. As soon as you enter it, you will find a donkey tied up. Its foal will be with it. Untie them and bring them both to me."
If someone tells you something, say that the Lord needs them. The Master will send them immediately.
This happened so that what was spoken by the prophet would be fulfilled. He says:
Say to Zion: Behold, the king will come to you, humble and riding on an ass, and on a young ass.
The disciples went and did what Jesus had instructed them to do.
Bring a donkey and its foal. They put their clothes on them. Then Jesus sat on the clothes.
A very large crowd gathered and spread their coats on the road. Others cut branches from the trees and spread them on the road.
Some of the people went ahead of him, and they followed, shouting, "Hosanna to the Son of David!" "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!" (Psalm 118:26) "Hosanna in the highest!"
When Jesus entered Jerusalem, the whole city shook. And the people asked, "Who is this?"
The multitude answered, 'This is Jesus, the prophet from Nazareth in Galilee.' Jesus purifies the temple.
Jesus entered the temple area. He began to drive out all those who were buying and selling there. He overturned the tables of those who were exchanging money. He also overturned the seats of those who were selling doves.
He said to them, "It is written, 'The Lord has said, "My house shall be called a house of prayer for all the peoples."' (Isaiah 56:7) And you are making it a den of robbers." (Jeremiah 7:11)
The blind and lame came to Jesus at the temple, and he healed them there.
The chief priests and the teachers of the law saw the wonderful things he did. They saw him heal many who had all kinds of diseases. The crippled and lame walked. People who had demons living inside them were healed. The crowds were amazed at what he did. When the chief priests and the teachers of the law saw the wonderful things he did and the children shouting in the temple, "Hosanna to the Son of David!" they were indignant.
"Do you hear what these children are saying?" they asked him. "Yes," Jesus answered. "Have you never read in the Scriptures: 'The Lord your God is the one who gives you the ability to do things. He shows you what to do, just as he showed those who served him long ago. You yourselves saw what these people did; you saw their mighty acts and the signs and wonders the Lord your God performed among you. You lived in the midst of that great assembly of people, a mighty army, which the Lord your God had for you, to keep you alive in every place you went."' (Deuteronomy 29: 10-13)
Then Jesus left the crowds, went out of the city to Bethany, and stayed there. The Withering of the Fig Tree
Early in the morning, Jesus was going back to Jerusalem. He was hungry.
He saw a fig tree by the roadside. He went to it, but found nothing on it but leaves. Then he said to it, "Never bear fruit again!" And immediately the fig tree withered.
When the disciples saw this, they were amazed. They asked, "How did the fig tree wither so quickly?"
Jesus answered, "Truly, I tell you: If you have faith, you will not doubt. But say to this mountain, 'Be lifted up and thrown into the sea,' and it will be done."
"If you believe, you will receive whatever you ask for when you pray." Jesus' authority is being questioned.
Jesus entered the temple court. As he taught there, the chief priests and the elders of the people came to him. They asked, "By what authority are you doing these things?" And "Who gave you this authority?"
Jesus answered, "I will also ask you one question. If you answer me, I will tell you by what authority I do these things."
From what source was John's baptism? Was it from heaven, or from men?" They were discussing this with one another. And they said, "If we say, 'From heaven,' he will say, 'Then why did you not believe him?'"
But what if we say: "Of the people"? We fear people. Everyone believes that John was a prophet."
They answered him, "Jesus, we do not know." Jesus said to them, "Then I will also tell you no longer by what authority I do these things." The parable of the two sons.
What do you think about this? A man had two sons. He went to the first one and said, "My son, go and work today in the vineyard."
The son said, 'I will not go.' But he later changed his mind and went.
Then the father went to the other son. He told him the same thing. The son replied, "Yes, sir." But he did not go.
Which of the two sons did what his father wanted?" They answered, "The first." Jesus said to them, "I tell you the truth: tax collectors and prostitutes are going ahead of you into the kingdom of God."
John came to show you the true way of life. And you did not believe in him. But the tax collectors and prostitutes believed. You have seen this. But even then you did not repent of your sins and believe in him. The story of the tenants.
Listen to another story. There was a man who owned a piece of land, so he planted a vineyard on it. And he surrounded it with a fence. And he dug a winepress in it. And he also built a watchtower. Then he rented the vineyard to farmers. And after that, he went on a journey.
When the harvest was near, he sent his servants to the tenants. He instructed the servants to collect his share of the fruits.
But the tenants seized his servants. They beat one of them. They killed another. They stoned the third to death.
Then the man sent other servants to the tenants. He sent more than he had sent at first. The tenants treated them in the same way.
Finally, he sent them his son. He said, "They will respect my son."
But the tenants watched for the son to come. And they said to each other, 'This is the heir. Come, let's kill him and the inheritance will be ours.'
So they seized him and cast him outside the vineyard. Then they killed him.
When the owner of the vineyard returns, what will he do to those tenants?
They said, "These wicked ones will destroy the vineyard.' 'Then the owner will rent the vineyard to other tenants, who will give him his share of the crop at harvest time."
Jesus said to them, "Have you not read what was written: 'The stone which the builders rejected has become the head of the corner. This was from the Lord, and it is amazing in our eyes' (Psalm 118: 22-23)?'"
So this is what I say to you. The kingdom of God will be taken from you, and given to a people who will produce its fruits.
Whoever falls on this stone will be broken to pieces. But the stone will crush whoever falls on it."
The chief priests and the Pharisees heard Jesus' parables. And they understood that he was speaking about them.
So they looked for a way to arrest him. But they feared the crowds. The people believed that Jesus was a prophet.
The Wedding Dinner Story
Jesus said other parables to them. And he said,
Here is how the Kingdom of Heaven is like. A king prepared a feast for his son.
He sent his servants to those invited to the dinner. The servants told them of their attendance. But they refused.
Then he sent out more servants. And he said, "Tell the invited guests, 'I have prepared my dinner. I have killed my ox and fattened pigs. Everything is ready. Come to the wedding feast.'""
But the people paid no attention to it. One of them went off to his field. And another went off to his work.
The rest seized his servants, treated them badly, and then killed them.
The king was furiously angry. He sent his army to destroy them. They killed those murderers and burned their city.
Then the king said to his servants, "The wedding feast is ready. But those I invited were not worthy to come."
Go into the street corners. Invite to dinner anyone you find.
The servants went out into the streets and gathered all the people they could find, both good and bad alike. Soon the wedding hall was filled with guests.
The king entered to see the guests. He noticed a man there who was not dressed in the wedding clothes.
"My friend," he asked, "how did you get in here without your wedding clothes?" The man could not find what to say.
Then the king said to his servants, "Bind his hands and feet, and throw him outside into the darkness. There people will cry and grind their teeth."
Many are called, but few are chosen. Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar?
So the Pharisees went out and conspired to trap Jesus in his words.
They sent their disciples to him. They sent the Herodians with them. And they said, "Teacher, we know that you are a truthful man. You teach the way of God in truth. No one dictates to you what you should do or say. You do not concern yourself with anyone's status."
Tell us then, what do you think; is it right to give tribute to Caesar, or not?"
But Jesus knew their evil schemes. He said, "You hypocrites! Why are you trying to trap me?"
"Show me the coin that people use to pay tax." They brought him a silver piece.
He asked them, "Whose picture is this? And whose words?"
"Caesar," they said. Then he said to them, "Give to Caesar what is Caesar's, and to God what is God's."
When they heard this, they were astonished. Then they left him and went away. Marriage is at the resurrection of the dead.
On that same day, the Pharisees came to Jesus and asked him a question. They do not believe in the resurrection of the dead.
They said, "Teacher, this is what Moses instructed: If a man dies without having children, his brother must marry the widow. He must have children to carry on his brother's name."
There were seven brothers among us. The first one got married and died. Since he had no children, he left his wife for his brother.
The same thing happened to the second and third brothers. The same thing happened to the seventh brother.
Finally, the woman died.
"Now, when the dead are raised, to whom will she be wife? All seven had been married to her."
Jesus answered, "You are wrong, because you do not know the Scriptures, nor the power of God."
When the dead are raised, they will not marry, nor will their parents give them in marriage. They will be like the angels in heaven.
What about the dead who rise? Have you not read what God has told you?
"I am the God of your father Abraham. I am the God of Isaac, and I am the God of Jacob." (Exodus 3:6) "He is not the God of the dead, but of the living."
When the crowds heard this, they were amazed at his teaching. The greatest commandment.
The Pharisees heard that the Sadducees could not answer Jesus. So the Pharisees gathered together.
One of them was an expert in the law. Jesus asked him a question to test him.
«Teacher," he asked, "which commandment is the most important in the Law?"»
Jesus answered: 'You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your mind.' (Deuteronomy 6: 5)
This is the first and most important commandment.
And a similar one: "Love your neighbor as yourself." (Leviticus 19:18)
All that was written in the law and the prophets depends on these two commandments." Who is the Son of Christ?
The Pharisees met. And Jesus asked them,
What do you think about the Christ? Whose son is he? And they answered, "The son of David."
He said to them, "Why then does David call him 'Lord'? The Holy Spirit spoke through David himself. David said,"
The Lord said to my Lord, "Sit at my right hand until I make your enemies your footstool." (Psalm 110:1)
If David was calling him Lord, how could he be his son?
No one could answer him with a single word. And from that day on, no one dared to ask him any other question.
Jesus judges the Pharisees and the Mosaic teachers.
Jesus spoke to the crowds and to his disciples.
He said, "The scribes and Pharisees sit on the seat of Moses."
Therefore, you must obey them. Do everything they ask of you. But do not do what they do. They do not practice what they preach.
They tie heavy loads and place them on the shoulders of others, but they themselves do not wish to move a finger to move them.
All they do they do so that people can see it. They carry on their foreheads and arms small boxes containing verses from the Holy Book. They make these boxes very wide. And they make the tassels on their coats very long.
They love to sit in a place of honor at banquets. They also love to have the most important seats in churches.
They like to be greeted in the markets. They like people to call them "Rabbi".
But do not call yourselves 'Rabbi.' You have only one teacher, and you are all brothers.
Do not call anyone on earth your father. You have one Father, and He is in heaven.
You should not be called 'Teacher.' You have one teacher, the Christ.
The most important person among you will be your servant.
He who exalts himself shall be abased; and he who abases himself shall be exalted.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you shut the kingdom of heaven against men. For you neither enter into the kingdom of heaven, nor allow those who are entering to go in.
.<.>
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you cross sea and land to make a single convert, and if you succeed, you make them twice as much a child of hell as yourselves.
Woe to you, guides of the blind! You say, 'If anyone swears by the temple, there is nothing wrong with him.' But whoever swears by the gold of the temple, he must fulfill his vow.'
You are blind and ignorant! Which is more important? The gold? Or the temple that sanctifies the gold?
And you also say, "If anyone takes an oath by the altar, it is nothing; but if anyone takes an oath by the gift that is on it, he must be bound by his oath."
Blind guides! Which is greater: the gift or the altar that sanctifies the gift?
Therefore, whoever swears by the altar, swears by it and by everything on it.
He who swears by the temple's right hand swears by its name and by the name of the one who dwells in it.
And he who swears by the sky, he swears by the throne of God and by Him who sat upon it.
Woe to you, teachers of the law and you, Pharisees, hypocrites! You tithe mint, dill and cumin, but you have neglected the more important matters of the law: justice, mercy and faithfulness. You should have done these things without neglecting the others.
O you blind guides, who are also blind! You strain out a gnat from your drink, but swallow a whole camel!
Woe to you, teachers of the law and you, Pharisees, you hypocrites! You are like whitewashed tombs, which look beautiful on the outside but are full of decay and death on the inside. You outwardly appear righteous to others, but within you, you are full of hypocrisy and lawlessness.
You blind Pharisee! First cleanse the inside of the cup and of the dish; and then cleanse the outside of them also.
Woe to you, teachers of the law and you, Pharisees, you hypocrites! You are like whitewashed tombs, which look beautiful on the outside but inside are full of the bones of the dead and all sorts of unclean and unpopular things.
With you, it's the same. From the outside, you appear to be doing the right thing. But on the inside, you are full of what is wrong. You pretend that you are not who you are.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you build the tombs of the prophets and decorate the tombs of the righteous.
And you say, "If we had been the ones before us, we would not have done what they did, we would not have helped in the killing of the prophets."
You testify against yourselves. You acknowledge that you are the children of those who killed the prophets.
Complete the iniquities that those before you began.
O nest of vipers! How can you escape the judgment of hell?
Therefore, I am sending you prophets and wise men and teachers. You will kill some of them, and you will crucify some of them, and others you will flog in your synagogues and you will persecute them from city to city.
You will pay the price for the blood of all the righteous, which was spilled on the earth. I am referring from the blood of righteous Abel to the blood of Zechariah son of Berechiah. Zechariah is the one you killed between the temple and the altar.
What I am about to say is the truth. All this will happen to those who are living now.
Oh Jerusalem, oh Jerusalem, you are killing the prophets and stoning those who have been sent to you. How often I have wanted to gather your children together as a hen gathers her brood under her wings, but you were not willing!
Look, your house was left empty.
I tell you: You will not see me henceforth, until you say: 'Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord.' (Psalm 118:26)
Signs of the Hour
Jesus left the temple. As he was walking away, his disciples came to him. They wanted to call his attention to the temple buildings.
"Do you see all these things?" Jesus asked. "Truly I tell you, not one stone here will be left on another; every one of them will be thrown down."
Jesus was sitting on the Mount of Olives. There, the disciples came to him privately. They said, "Tell us, when will this be? And what will be the sign of your coming? And what will be the sign of the end of the age?"
Jesus answered, "Beware! Be careful that no one deceives you."
Many will come in my name and will say, 'I am the Messiah!' and they will lead many astray.
You will hear about wars. And you will also hear about people talking about future wars. Do not panic. Those things must happen. But the end is not yet come.
Nation will fight against nation, and kingdom against kingdom. There will be great earthquakes, famines and pestilences in many places.
This is all the beginning of labor.
Then they will deliver you over to be afflicted and killed, and you will be hated by all nations because of me.
At that time, many will fall away from the faith, and they will hate one another, and many will betray one another.
Many false prophets will appear. And they will lead many people astray.
Because evil is increasing, the love of most people will grow cold.
But the one who endures to the end, he will be saved.
This good news of the kingdom will be preached in the whole world as a testimony to all nations. Then the end will come.
The prophet Daniel spoke of "the detestable thing that causes desolation." (Daniel 9:27; 11:31; 12:11) Some day you will see it standing in the holy place. Let the reader understand this.
Let those who are in Judea flee to the mountains.
No one on the surface should go down to their home to get anything.
No one in the field should go back to get their coat.
What an ordeal it was for pregnant women in those days! What an ordeal it was for nursing mothers!
Pray so that you don't have to flee in winter or on a Saturday.
There will be great tribulation in those days; it will be worse than any other tribulation since the world began until now, and there will be nothing like it ever again.
If time were not shortened, no one would survive. But because of the elect people of God, it will be shortened.
In those days, someone might say to you, 'Here is the Messiah!' or 'There he is!' Do not believe it.
False messiahs and false prophets will appear, performing great signs and wonders, and they will try to deceive the elect people of God, if possible.
Look, I already told you.
Therefore, if someone tells you, "He is far in the desert," do not go there. Or if someone says, "He is deep inside the house," do not believe him.
The lightning that comes from the east is seen in the west. So will the coming of the Son of Man be.
Vultures will gather wherever there is a carcass.
Immediately after the terrible suffering of those days, "the sun is darkened, and the moon does not give its light, and the stars are fallen from heaven, and the powers of the heavens are shaken." (Isaiah 13:10; 34:4)
Then the sign of the Son of Man will appear in heaven, and all the nations of the earth will mourn. They will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven with power and great glory.
He will send His angels with a great trumpet sound. He will gather His chosen servants from all over the earth. He will come to them from one end of the heavens to the other.
Learn a lesson from the fig tree. When its branches become thin and it puts out leaves, you know that summer is near.
And in the same way, when you see all those things happening, you know that the end is near. It is about to happen.
What I am telling you is true. The people alive at that time will certainly not die until all those things have happened.
The heaven and the earth shall pass away, but my words shall not pass away. No one knows the day or the hour.
No one knows about that day or hour, not even the angels in heaven. And no one knows the Son except the Father, and the Son to whom the Father reveals him.
Remember how it was in the days of Noah. So also will the coming of the Son of Man be.
In the days before the flood, people were eating and drinking. They were marrying. They were giving their daughters in marriage. They did all those things until the day that Noah entered the ark.
They did not know anything about what would happen until the flood came and took them all. So it will be when the Son of Man comes.
Two men will be in the field. One will be taken, and the other will be left.
Two women will be grinding with a hand mill; one will be taken and the other will be left.
So watch yourselves; you do not know on what day your Lord will come.
You must understand something. Let's suppose the homeowner knew at what hour of the night the thief would come. Then he would have watched and watched. He would not have allowed his house to be violated.
So you too, be ready. For the Son of Man is coming at an hour when you do not expect.
Let us suppose that a master entrusted one of his slaves with the responsibility of overseeing the rest of the servants in his house. The slave's task is to provide them with food at the proper time. The master wants a slave who is faithful and wise for this position.
It would be good for a slave for his master to see him engaged in his work upon the master's return.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. That servant will be put in charge of managing everything his master owns by that master's appointment.
But let us suppose that this slave is evil. Let us suppose that he says to himself: "My master's absence will be long."
Let's suppose he starts hitting the other servants. And let's suppose he eats and drinks with those who drink too much.
The master of that slave will come on a day that the slave does not think he will come on, and at an hour that the slave does not know.
Then the master himself will chop him to pieces. And he will send him to the place where the protesters go. There the people will weep and grind their teeth.
The story of the ten brides.
Here is how the Kingdom of Heaven will be at that time. Ten virgins took their lamps and went out to meet the bridegroom.
Five of them were foolish. And five were wise.
The foolish took their lamps, but did not take oil with them.
The wise ones took oil in jars with their lamps.
The bridegroom came not for a long time. So, the bridesmaids all got tired and slept.
In the middle of the night, someone shouted: "Here is the bridegroom! Come to meet him!"
Then all the bridesmaids woke up and prepared their lamps.
The foolish said to the wise, "Give us of your oil, for our lamps are going out."
They said: 'No', it might not be enough for everyone. Rather, go to the sellers and buy for yourselves.
So they went to buy oil. But while they were on their way, the bridegroom arrived. And the bridesmaids, who were ready with him, went in to the wedding supper. Then the door was shut.
Later, the other bridesmaids came, too. "Sir! Sir!" they said. "Open the door for us!"
But he answered, "What I am going to tell you is the truth. I do not know you."
Therefore, be you watchful, not knowing when that Bridegroom cometh. The Parable of the Three Servants.
And again, thus is the Kingdom of Heaven. A certain man was traveling on a journey, and he called his servants and entrusted to them his property.
He gave $10,000 to one. And he gave $4,000 to another. And he gave $2,000 to the third. He had given the man each servant the amount he knew the servant could care for. Then he set out on his journey.
The servant who received the ten thousand dollars went immediately and put his money to work. And he earned another ten thousand dollars.
The one who won 40,000 dollars won an additional 40,000 dollars.
But the man who received two thousand dollars went and dug a hole in the ground. He hid his master's money there.
After a long time, the master of those servants returned. He wanted to gather all the money that they had earned.
The man who received $10,000 brought the other $10,000. He said, "Sir, you entrusted me with $10,000. Look, I have earned another $10,000."
His master answered him, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful in a few things; I will put you in charge of many things. Come and share your master's joy!"
The man who had 40,000 dollars also came. He said, "Master, you trusted me with 40,000 dollars. Look, I have earned another 40,000 dollars."
His master answered him, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful in a few things; I will put you in charge of many things. Come and share your master's joy!"
Then the man who had taken the two thousand came. He said, "Master, I have learned that you are a strict man. You gather where you did not sow, and you gather a harvest where you did not scatter seed."
I was afraid. I went out and hid two thousand dollars for you in the ground. Look, this is what belongs to you.
His master answered him, 'You wicked, lazy servant! So you knew that I harvest where I did not sow, and gather where I did not scatter?
Well, you should have deposited my money in the bank. When I came back, I would have withdrawn it with interest.
Then his master ordered the other servants: "Take the 2000 dollars from him. Give it to the one who has 20000 dollars."
To him who has, more shall be given, so that he will have an abundance. But from the one who has nothing, even what he has will be taken away.
Drive out that monstrous servant to the outside. There in the darkness they cry and beg, gnawing their teeth.' The sheep and the goats
The Son of Man will come in His glory, and all His angels with Him. Then He will sit on His glorious throne. And before Him will be gathered
All nations will be gathered before him. He will separate people into two groups. It will be like a shepherd separating the sheep from the goats.
He will place the sheep on his right and the goats on his left.
Then the King will speak with those on his right, saying, "Blessed Father, you have blessed them. Come and receive what has been prepared for you, the King who has been prepared for you since the foundation of the world."
I was hungry, and you fed me. I was thirsty, and you gave me drink. I was a stranger, and you took me in.
I needed clothes. And you gave them to me. I was sick. And you took care of me. I was in prison. And you came to visit me.
Then the people who did the good deeds will answer, saying, 'Lord, when did we see you hungry and feed you? And when did we see you thirsty and give you a drink?'
When did we ever see you as a stranger and not invite you in? When did we ever see you in need of clothes and not clothe you?
When did we see you sick or in prison so we could visit you?
The King will reply, 'The very words I have spoken to you are the Truth. Whatever you did for one of the least of these brothers of mine, you did for me.'
Then He will say to those on His left, 'Cursed ones! Go away from Me to the everlasting burning fire, which has been prepared for the devil and his angels.'
I was hungry. But you did not give me anything to eat. I was thirsty. But you did not give me anything to drink.
I was a stranger. But you did not invite me in. I needed clothes. But you did not give me anything. I was sick and in prison. But you did not take care of me.
And they will also answer: 'Lord, when did we see you hungry or thirsty and did not help you? And when did we see you as a stranger or needing clothes or sick or in prison and did not help you?'
He will reply, 'Truly I tell you, just as you did it to one of the least of these, you did it to me.'
Then they will go to eternal punishment. As for those who did good deeds, they will receive eternal life.
The plan to kill Jesus.
Jesus said all these things. Then he said to his disciples:
You know, Easter is in two days. And the Son of Man is to be handed over to be crucified.
Then the chief priests and the elders of the people assembled at the palace of Caiaphas, the high priest.
They made plans to arrest Jesus by some subtle means. They wanted to kill him.
"But not during the feast," they said. "It might stir up the people." A woman pours perfume on Jesus.
Jesus was in Bethany. He was in the house of a man named Simon, who had a skin disease.
A woman came to Jesus with an alabaster jar of very costly perfume. She poured it on his head as he reclined at the table.
When the disciples saw this, they were angry. They asked, "What is the purpose of this waste?"
The perfume could have been sold for a high price. The money could have been donated to the poor.
Jesus was aware of this. So he said to them, "Why do you trouble this woman? For she has done a beautiful thing to me."
The poor you will always have with you, but you will not always have me.
She poured perfume on me to prepare me for burial.
What I am determined to tell you is the truth. What I have done will be told everywhere the good news about this wonderful gospel is preached all over the world. It will be told in connection with it." Judas agrees to hand Jesus over.
From the twelve, he went to the chief priests. His name was Judas Iscariot.
And he asked, "What will you give me if I hand Jesus over to you?" And they set up for him thirty pieces of silver.
Since then, Judas watched for an opportunity to hand Jesus over to them. The Last Supper.
That day was the first day of Unleavened Bread. The disciples came to Jesus and asked Him, saying, "Where do You want us to prepare for You to eat the Passover?"
He answered, "Go into the city to a certain man who is my disciple. And say to him, 'The Teacher says, My time is at hand. I am going to keep the Passover at your house with my disciples.'"
So the disciples did what Jesus had instructed them to do. And they prepared the Passover meal.
And when evening came, Jesus was reclining at the table with the twelve disciples.
While they were eating, he said, "What I am about to tell you is the truth. One of you will betray me to my enemies."
The disciples were deeply grieved. And they began one after another to ask him, saying, "Are you not the Christ, the Son of God?"
Jesus answered, "The one who dipped his hand with me in the bowl is the one who will betray me."
The Son of Man will go away, just as it is written about him. But woe to that man by whom the Son of Man is betrayed! It would have been better for him if he had never been born."
It was Judas who was going to betray him. He said, "Surely it is not I, Master?" And Jesus answered, "Yes. It is you."
While they were eating, Jesus took bread. And when He had given thanks, He broke it and gave it to the disciples. And He said to them, "Take, eat; this is My body."
Then he took the cup. Thanking, he handed it over to them. And he said, "Drink from it all of you."
This is my blood of the New Covenant. It was poured out for the forgiveness of the sins of many.
Here is what I say to you. From now on, I will not drink this fruit of the vine until the day when I drink it with you new in the kingdom of my Father."
Then they sang a hymn and went out to the Mount of Olives. Jesus says that Peter will fail.
Jesus said to them, "This very night all of you will desert me. For it is written, 'The Lord said to me, "I will strike down the shepherd, and the sheep will be scattered."' (Zechariah 13:7)
But after I am raised from the dead, I will go ahead of you to Galilee."
Peter answered, "All the others on account of you might abandon him, but I will never abandon him."
"What I am about to tell you is the truth," Jesus answered. "This very night, before the rooster crows, you will deny me three times and tell me you do not know me."
But Peter said, "I will have to die with you. I will never disown you." All the other disciples said the same thing. Jesus Prays in Gethsemane.
Then Jesus went with his disciples to a place called Gethsemane. He said to them, "Sit here, and I will go over there to pray."
Peter took with him James and John, the sons of Zebedee. And he began to be sorrowful and very heavy.
And He said to them, "My soul is very sorrowful, even to death. Stay here and watch with Me."
He went a little farther. Then he fell on his face. And he prayed, saying, "Father, if it be possible, let this cup pass from me. Nevertheless, not my will, but thine, be done."
Then he came back to his disciples and found them sleeping. He asked Peter, "Could you not watch with me for one hour?"
Watch and pray. Then you will not fall into temptation when you are put to the test. The Spirit is willing, but the flesh is weak.
Jesus went out again. And he prayed, saying, "Father, if it be possible, let this cup pass from me; nevertheless, not as I will, but as Thou wilt."
Then he came back. And he found them sleeping again. They couldn't keep their eyes open.
So he left them and went away again. And for a third time he prayed the same prayer.
Then he returned to the disciples. And he said to them, "Are you still sleeping and taking your rest? Behold, the hour is already late! The Son of Man is about to be betrayed into the hands of sinners."
Rise! Let us go! Here is he that delivered me unto them!» Jesus was arrested.
While Jesus was speaking, Judas came. He was one of the twelve. A large crowd was with him. They were carrying swords and clubs. The chief priests and elders of the people had sent them.
Judas, who was going to betray Jesus, had arranged a signal with them. He said, "The one I kiss is the one; seize him."
Immediately Judas went to Jesus and said, "Greetings, Rabbi!" And he kissed him.
Jesus answered, "My friend, do as you have come to do." Then the men approached. And they seized Jesus and arrested him.
At that moment, one of Jesus' disciples drew his sword. He struck the servant of the high priest, cutting off his ear.
Jesus said to him, "Put your sword back into its place. For all who take the sword will perish by the sword."
Do you think I can't ask my father for help? He will immediately send an army of more than 70,000 kings.
But then how are the holy books fulfilled? They say it must happen this way."
At that time Jesus spoke to the crowds, "Am I leading an armed rebellion against you?" he asked. "Must I come to you with swords and clubs to arrest me? For I used to sit daily in the temple teaching, and you did not arrest me."
But all this was to fulfill the words of the prophets.' Then all the disciples left Jesus and fled. Jesus is taken to the Sanhedrin.
Those who arrested Jesus led him to Caiaphas the high priest. The scribes and elders were assembled there.
Not long ago, Peter followed Jesus. He even went to the courtyard of the high priest. He entered and sat with the guards to see what would happen.
The chief priests and the entire Sanhedrin were searching for something they could use against Jesus. They wanted to kill him.
But they found no evidence, although many false witnesses came forward. Finally, two more witnesses came forward.
They said, "This man has been claiming, 'I am able to destroy the temple of God. And I am able to rebuild it in three days.' "
Then the high priest rose and questioned Jesus, saying, "Are you not going to answer? What are these charges that these men are bringing against you?"
But Jesus remained silent. Then the high priest said, "I adjure you by the living God, tell us if you are the Christ, the Son of God."
Yes. As you have well said, Jesus answered. But this is what I say to all of you. In the coming days, you will see the Son of Man seated at the right hand of the Power. You will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven.
Then the high priest tore his clothes and said, "He has spoken a great sin against the Lord! Do we need any more witnesses? For you have heard his blasphemy."
"What do you think?" they answered: "He must die!"
Then they spat in his face. And they hit him with cuffs. And others slapped him.
They said, "Tell us, O Christ! Who is it that smote thee?" Peter says that he does not know Jesus.
Peter was sitting in the courtyard. A servant girl came to him and said, "You also were with Jesus the Galilean."
But before them all, Peter said it was not so. "I don't know what you are talking about," he said.
Then he went out to the gate leading to the courtyard. There another woman saw him. She said to the people, "This man was with Jesus of Nazareth."
And again he said it was not so. He swore, "I do not know this man!"
Soon, those who were standing there drew near to Peter; they said, "Surely you are one of them; for the way you speak shows it."
Then Peter began to curse and swear, and he said to them, "I do not know this man!" And immediately the cock crowed.
Peter remembered what Jesus had said to him, "Before the rooster crows, you will deny me three times." And so, Peter went out and wept bitterly.
Yehuda commits suicide by hanging.
This was in the early morning. The chief priests and the elders of the people all resolved to kill Jesus.
They bound him and led him away. Then they handed him over to Pilate, the governor.
Judas, who had betrayed him, seeing that Jesus was condemned to death, felt great remorse and deep regret for what he had done. He went back to the chief priests and the elders and returned the thirty pieces of silver.
"I made a mistake," he said. "I handed over an innocent man." "What do we care?" they replied. "This is your problem."
Then Judas threw the silver into the temple and went away. Then he went and hanged himself.
The high priests picked up the coins. "We cannot put this money into the temple treasury," they said. "It is blood money. It was paid for a man's death."
They decided to use the money to buy a field for strangers. People from other countries will be buried in it.
And for this reason, the Field of Blood has borne this name down to the present day.
Then the word of the prophet Jeremiah was fulfilled. For he had said, "They took the thirty pieces of silver, the price at which they valued him, from the people of Israel."
They took the silver and bought with it the potter's field, as the Lord had directed me. (Zechariah 11:12,13; Jeremiah 19:1-13; 32:6-9) And Jesus was brought before Pilate.
Jesus was standing before the governor. The governor asked him, "Are you the king of the Jews?" Jesus answered, "Yes, it is as you say."
But when the chief priests and elders accused him, he made no answer.
Pilate asked him, "Do you not hear the charges they are bringing against you?"
But Jesus answered nothing, not even to a single charge. And the governor was quite surprised.
It was the custom of the governor on Easter to release a certain prisoner; and the people had the power to choose whom they would.
In those days, there was a famous prisoner named Barabbas.
And when the multitudes were gathered together, Pilate saith unto them, Whom will ye that I release unto you? Barabbas or Jesus which is called Christ?
Pilate knew that the leaders were envious. He knew that this was the reason they had handed Jesus over to him.
While Pilate was sitting on the judgment seat, his wife sent him a message. She said, "Have nothing to do with that righteous man; for I have suffered many things this day in a dream because of him."
But the chief priests, the priests, and the elders of the people persuaded the crowds to ask for Barabbas to be released and for Jesus to be crucified.
"Which of these two do you want me to release?" asked the governor. "Barabbas," they answered.
Pilate said, "What shall I do with Jesus who is called Christ?" They all answered, "Let him be crucified!"
«Why? What evil has he done?» asked Pilate. But they cried out all the louder, «Crucify him!»
Pilate saw that he was accomplishing nothing. Instead, the anger of the crowd was growing. He took water and washed his hands in front of them. "I am innocent of this man's blood," he said. "You are the ones who bear the responsibility for it!"
Then all the people answered together, "His blood shall be on us and on our children!"
Pilate released Barabbas. But he ordered Jesus to be scourged. Then he handed him over to be crucified on the cross. Soldiers mock Jesus.
The governor's soldiers took Jesus to the palace called the Praetorium. The rest of the soldiers gathered around him.
They stripped him of his clothes and clothed him in a purple robe.
Then they twisted the thorns to make a crown. They put it on his head. And they put a reed in his right hand. Then they knelt down before him and mocked him. "Hail, King of the Jews!" they said.
They spat on him. They hit him on the head with a stick again and again.
After mocking him, they took off his robe. They put on him his own clothes. Then they took him to crucify him on the cross. Jesus is crucified on the cross.
And on their way out of the city, they met a man from Cyrene, named Simon. They forced him to carry the cross.
They came to a place called Golgotha. And the word Golgotha means the place of the skull.
There they mixed wine with bitter spices and gave it to Jesus to drink. And when he tasted it, he refused to drink.
And when they crucified him, they divided his garments among them by casting lots.
They sat there and watched him.
Above his head they put the tablet on which was written his accusation. It was written: "This is Jesus, King of the Jews."
And two thieves were crucified with him, one on his right and one on his left.
Those who passed by scoffed at Jesus and ridiculed him. They shook their heads.
And they said, "Are you to destroy the sanctuary and build it in three days? Save yourself! Come down from the cross, if you are the Son of God!"
And in the same way, the chief priests, the scribes, and the elders mocked him.
They said, "He saves others, but he cannot save himself! He is the King of Israel; let him come down now from the cross, and we will believe in him."
He trusts in God to deliver him now if he so wills. It is he who said, "I am the Son of God."
And in the same way, the thieves who were being crucified with Jesus also mocked him. Jesus' death.
From noon until three o'clock, darkness covered the entire earth.
About the third hour, Jesus cried out with a loud voice, " 'Eloi, Eloi, lama sabachthani?' " This means, " 'My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?' " (Psalm 22:1)
Some of those who were standing there heard Jesus cry out. And they said, "He is calling Elijah."
Immediately, one of them ran and brought a sponge. He filled it with sour wine and put it on a reed. And he offered it to Jesus to drink.
The rest said, "Leave him by himself. Let us see if Elijah will come to save him."
After Jesus also loudly screamed, he died.
At that moment, the veil of the temple was torn from top to bottom. And the earth shook. And the rocks were split.
The tombs were split open. And many of the saints who had died arose to life.
They came out of the tombs. And after the resurrection of Jesus, they went to the holy city. And there they appeared to many people.
The Roman centurion and the guards who were guarding Jesus saw the earthquake and all that had happened; and they were filled with fear, and said, "Truly he was the Son of God!"
It was not far, many women were watching. They had followed Jesus from Galilee to care for his needs.
Mary Magdalene was among them. Mary, the mother of James and Joseph, was there also. The mother of the sons of Zebedee was there also. Jesus was buried.
When evening came, a rich man from Aramathea came. His name was Joseph. He had become a disciple of Jesus.
He went to Pilate and asked for Jesus' body. Pilate ordered it to be handed over to him.
Joseph took the body and wrapped it in a clean linen shroud.
He placed him in his new tomb which he had carved out of the rock. Then he rolled a large stone in front of the entrance to the tomb. Then he left.
Mary Magdalene and the other Mary were sitting there opposite the tomb. The guards at the tomb.
The next day was the day after the preparation day. The chief priests and the Pharisees went to Pilate.
They said, 'Lord, we remember what that deceiver said while he was alive.' He claimed, 'After three days I will be raised.'
So he ordered the tomb to be made secure until the third day. If you do not, then his disciples may come and steal the body. Then they will go and tell the people that Jesus has risen from the dead. This last lie will be more damaging than the first."
Pilate said, "Take with you some guards. Go and make the tomb as secure as you can."
So they went and secured the lock of the tomb. They placed a seal on the stone, and they put some guards on duty.
Jesus rises from the dead.
Saturday has passed. And the dawn of the first day of the week has come. And Mary the Magdalene and the other Mary went to look at the tomb.
There was a great earthquake. The angel of the Lord descended from heaven. The angel went to the tomb. He rolled away the stone and sat on it.
His body shone like lightning. His clothes were white as snow.
The guards were so afraid of him that they trembled and became like the dead.
The angel said to the women, "Do not be afraid. I know that you are looking for Jesus, who was crucified."
He is not here! He has risen, just as he said! Come and see the place where he lay.
Go quickly! And tell his disciples, "He has been raised from the dead. He is going before you to Galilee. There you will see him." Now I have told you."
The women quickly left the tomb, for terror and great joy had seized them; and while they were running to give the disciples the glad tidings, suddenly Jesus met them and said, "Rejoice!"
Suddenly, Jesus met them. He said, "Peace be with you!" They approached him and held his feet and worshipped him.
Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid. Go and tell my brothers to go to Galilee. There they will see me." The guards' report to the chief priests.
While the women were on their way, some guards entered the city. They reported to the chief priests everything that had happened.
When the chief priests met with the elders, they made a plan. They gave a large sum of money to the soldiers.
They said to the soldiers: "We want you to say: 'His disciples came at night and stole his body while we were sleeping.'"
If the Governor hears this report, we will bribe him. This will protect you from trouble."
So the soldiers took the money and did what they were told to do. This story spread among the Jews to this day. Jesus' last commands to his disciples.
Then the eleven disciples went to Galilee. They went to the mountain to which Jesus had commanded them to go.
When they saw it, they worshipped it, but some of them still had doubts.
Then Jesus came to them and said, "All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me."
Go ye, therefore, and teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Ghost.
Teach them to obey everything I have commanded you. And you will be able to be certain that I am with you always, to the end of time."
John the Baptist is preparing the way of the Lord.
This is the beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God.
A long time ago, Isaiah the prophet wrote: "Behold, I send my messenger before your face, who shall prepare your way." (Malachi 3:1)
A voice cries out in the wilderness: "Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight."
And so John came. And he baptized people in the wilderness. And he also preached that people should be baptized and repent of their sins. Then God will forgive them.
All the people of the villages of Judea went out to him, and all the people of Jerusalem; and they confessed their sins, and he baptized them in the river Jordan.
John wore clothes made of camel's hair. He wore a leather belt around his waist. He ate locusts and wild honey.
This is what John used to preach: "After me comes the one who is mightier than I am. I am not worthy to stoop down and untie the thong of his sandals."
I baptize you with water; but he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." The Baptism and Temptation of Jesus.
In those days Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee, and John the Baptist baptized him in the Jordan River.
Jesus was just coming up out of the water. And at that moment he saw the heavens split open. And he saw the Holy Spirit descend on him like a dove.
A voice came from heaven, "You are my beloved Son; with you I am well pleased."
Immediately, the Spirit sent Jesus out into the wilderness.
He was in the wilderness for forty days. There the devil tempted him. Wild beasts did not harm Jesus. Angels looked after him. Jesus chooses the first disciples.
After John was imprisoned, Jesus went to Galilee. And he proclaimed the good news of God.
The time has come, he said. The kingdom of God is near. Therefore, repent of your sins and believe the Gospel!
One day, Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. There he saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
"Come, follow me," Jesus said. "I will make you fishers of men."
And immediately they left their nets and followed him.
Then Jesus walked another step. And while he was doing that, he saw James the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. They were in a boat preparing their nets.
He called them at once. They left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men, and then they followed Jesus. Jesus Expels a Demon.
Jesus and those who were with him went to Capernaum. And when the Sabbath came, he entered the synagogue. And there he began to teach.
The people were astonished at his teaching. He taught them with authority, not like the scribes.
And at that moment a man in their synagogue cried out, for he was possessed by an unclean spirit; and he said,
What do you have against us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are. You are the Holy One of God!
"Be quiet!" Jesus said firmly. "Come out of him!"
The man violently shuddered because of the evil. Then, a scream came out of him.
All the people were amazed. They asked each other, "What is this? A new teaching! And with authority! He commands even the unclean spirits, and they obey him!"
News about Jesus spread quickly throughout Galilee. Jesus heals many people.
Jesus and those who were with him went out of the synagogue. And immediately they went with James and John to the house of Simon and Andrew.
Simon's mother-in-law was lying in bed. She was suffering from a fever. So they told Jesus about her.
He went to her, took her hand and helped her to stand up. He cured her of the fever. Then she began to serve them.
And that evening, after sunset, the people brought to Jesus all who were sick. They also brought all who were possessed by demons.
All the people in the town gathered at the gate.
Jesus healed many of them. They were suffering from every kind of disease. And He also cast out many demons. But He would not allow the demons to speak, because they knew who He was. Jesus is praying in a quiet place.
It was very early in the morning and still dark. Jesus got up and went out of the house. He went to a place where he could be alone. There he prayed.
Simon and his friends went to look for Jesus.
When they found him, they called out: "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus answered, "Let us go to another place, so that I can go to the neighboring villages and preach there also. For this I have come."
He traveled throughout all of Galilee. He was teaching in their synagogues, and he was casting out demons. Jesus heals a man who was suffering from a skin disease.
A man with a skin disease came to Jesus. Falling on his knees, he begged Jesus. "If you want to make me 'clean,'" he said, "you can."
Jesus was filled with profound concern. He reached out and touched the man. He said, "I want to do this. Be pure!"
Immediately the disease left him. He was cured.
Jesus immediately sent him away and gave him a strong warning.
"Don't tell anyone about this," he said. "Go and present yourself to the priest. Offer the sacrifices that Moses commanded. This will serve as a testimony to the priest and the people that you are 'clean'."
But the man went out and began to speak at once. He spread the news to everyone. So Jesus was no longer able to enter a town publicly. He stayed outside in lonely places. But people still came to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who could not walk.
After a few days, Jesus entered Capernaum again. And it was heard that he had returned to the house.
The crowd of people gathered so much that there was no more room. There wasn't even any space outside the door. And Jesus was preaching the word to them.
Four of those who came were carrying a man who could not walk.
But they could not bring him to Jesus because of the crowd. So they dug an opening through the roof above him. Then they lowered the man on the stretcher through the opening.
Jesus saw their faith. And he said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
Some students of the law were sitting there. They were thinking,
Why does this man speak in this way? He is saying something very wicked indeed! God alone forgives sins!
And Jesus immediately knew what they were thinking. He asked them, "Why are you thinking these things?"
Is it easier for him to say to this man, "Your sins have been forgiven"? Or to say, "Rise, take up your bed and walk"?
"I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then Jesus spoke to the man who could not walk.
He said, "Rise, take your mat and go to your house."
The man got up and took a mat. Then he walked away while everyone was watching. All the people were amazed. They praised God and said, "We have never seen anything like this!" Jesus Chooses Levi
And Jesus went out again beside the Sea of Galilee. And a great multitude gathered unto him. And he began to teach them.
And as he walked, he saw Levi son of Alphaeus. And Levi was sitting in the tax office. And Jesus said to him, "Follow me." And Levi rose up and followed him.
Later, Jesus was having dinner at the home of Levi. Many tax collectors and "sinners" were eating with him and his disciples. They were part of the large crowds that were following Jesus.
There were some Pharisee teachers. They saw him eating with "sinners" and tax collectors. So they asked his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and 'sinners'?"
Jesus heard that. And he said to them: "Those who are healthy do not need a doctor, the sick are the ones who need him. I did not come to call the righteous, but to call sinners." Jesus was asked about fasting.
The disciples of John and the Pharisees were fasting. Some people came to Jesus and said to him, "The disciples of John fast, and the disciples of the Pharisees fast, but your disciples do not fast. Why do your disciples not fast?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom fast as long as he is with them? They cannot fast as long as he is with them."
But the day will come when the bridegroom is taken from them. On that day they go without food.
People don't patch a piece of new cloth onto old clothes. If they did, the new piece would shrink from the old. That would make the tear even worse.
People don't pour new wine into old wine skins. If they do, the wine will break the skins. Then the wine and the skins are both lost. Instead, everyone pours new wine into new wineskins." Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
On a Saturday, Jesus was walking through the fields with his disciples. The disciples began to pick the heads of grain.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Behold, it is forbidden to do this on the Sabbath. Why then do your disciples do this?"
He answered, saying, "Have you never read about the deed of David? He and his men were hungry. They needed food."
That was when Ahitab was the high priest. David went into the house of God and ate the bread of presence. No one except the priests was allowed to eat it. David gave some of it to his men as well.
Jesus said to them, "The Sabbath was made for man, not man for the Sabbath."
So the Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath."
Healing on the Sabbath.
Now on another occasion Jesus went into the synagogue. And there was a man there whose hand was withered and deformed.
Some Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. They watched him closely. They wanted to see if he would heal a man on the Sabbath day.
Jesus spoke to the man who had a withered and deformed hand. He said, "Stand in front of everyone."
Jesus asked them, "What does the Law say? What must we do on the Sabbath? Is it right to do good or evil? Is it right to save life or to destroy it?" But no one answered him.
Jesus looked around at them angrily. He was very upset because their hearts were hardened. Then He said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." So he stretched it out, and his hand was restored as if it had never been deficient.
Then the Pharisees went out and began to plot with the Herodians against Jesus, seeking to kill him. The crowds follow Jesus.
Jesus and His disciples went to the Sea of Galilee. A great multitude from Galilee followed Him.
People heard about all the things Jesus was doing. Many people came to him. They came from Judea and Jerusalem and Idumea. And they came from the territory east of the Jordan River. And they came from the area of Tyre and Sidon.
And because of the crowds, Jesus asked his disciples to prepare a small boat for him. This was to prevent people from crowding him.
Jesus healed many people. The sick people themselves paid to touch him.
When the unclean spirits saw Jesus, they fell down before him. And the spirits cried out, saying, "You are the Son of God!"
But Jesus commanded them not to tell who he was. Jesus appoints the twelve apostles.
Jesus went up to a mountain. He called a certain people to come to him, and they came.
He appointed twelve of them and called them apostles. And from that time they were with him. And he also sent them out to preach.
And they had the authority to drive out demons.
Jesus made it a practice to appoint twelve apostles. Simon was one of them. Jesus gave him the name Peter.
There was Jacob the son of Zebedee and his brother John. Jesus gave them the name Sons of Thunder. And Sons of Thunder means Children of Thunder.
And Andrew and Philip and Bartholomew and Matthew and Thomas and James the son of Alphaeus. And there were also Thaddaeus and Simon the Zealot.
Judas Iscariot was one of them too. He was the one who would later betray Jesus into the hands of his enemies. Jesus and Beelzebub.
Jesus entered a house. A large crowd also gathered. The crowd was so large that Jesus and his disciples couldn't even eat.
His family heard about this. So they went to take care of him. They said, "He has lost his mind."
There were some who taught the law. They came down from Jerusalem. They said, "He is driving out demons by the power of the chief demon, Beelzebul."
Jesus called them and spoke to them in parables. He said, "How can Satan cast out Satan?"
If a kingdom fights against itself, it will not last.
If a family is divided, it cannot last.
